  "text": {
    "name": "Arab Spring",
    "rules": "Record \"the square\". Each side chooses: Back the protesters? If LIO says yes, (draw from the Contingency deck of the side that played this card, then if the result is 3 or more, LIO spends 1 point on actions, then until the end of this turn, each time LIO spends points, it spends 1 fewer, never below 1; otherwise, until the end of this turn, each time LIO spends points, it spends 1 fewer, never below 1), then (if SAE says yes, (draw from the Contingency deck of the side that played this card, then if the result is 3 or more, SAE spends 1 point on actions, then until the end of this turn, each time SAE spends points, it spends 1 fewer, never below 1; otherwise, until the end of this turn, each time SAE spends points, it spends 1 fewer, never below 1); otherwise, at the end of this turn, SAE spends 1 point placing influence); otherwise, (if SAE says yes, (draw from the Contingency deck of the side that played this card, then if the result is 3 or more, SAE spends 1 point on actions, then until the end of this turn, each time SAE spends points, it spends 1 fewer, never below 1; otherwise, until the end of this turn, each time SAE spends points, it spends 1 fewer, never below 1), then at the end of this turn, LIO spends 1 point placing influence).",
    "history": "Protests that began in Sidi Bouzid, Tunisia in December 2010 spread across the Arab world; Tunisia's president fled on 14 January 2011, Egypt's resigned on 11 February 2011, and Libya's was killed in October 2011.",
